1. Behaviour vs upstream. async_iter / async_for against lib.js asyncIter / asyncFor called directly in node: identical call order and arguments in 16 scenarios (sync, empty, deferred next, next called twice, next after the end, early done, array growing during the loop, iterator throwing, next identity). HEAD modules vs this tree: identical traces in all 27 scenarios incl. waterfall. __SUB__ is used directly in the step sub at all three sites (never inside a nested sub), so it is the same coderef $next held. Deferred use: a task / iterator that keeps the callback and calls it after the function returned completes correctly, and nothing is freed early -- 13 template shapes rendered through the callback API with an async filter that defers (include, asyncEach, asyncAll, extends + super, three levels), Environment and context dropped before the queue is drained: output, error and filter call order equal upstream (node) and HEAD. Callbacks are released afterwards (DESTROY markers: 2/2 freed in 10 sync / deferred / abandoned / error / die cases; 0/2 at HEAD).

